judgings-answered-smaller: 2
-----
<time>	judgings_answered_smaller	<project> because no tree of this checkout is held and none is being read, so only the rules that answer from one file will ever run here
<time>	judgings_answered_smaller	<project> because no tree of this checkout is held and none is being read, so only the rules that answer from one file will ever run here
-----
Code-commandments — what the judging service has cost this project: every read of a whole tree and every judging it answered, counted, so a test or a person can see whether a run read the tree or waited for it.

The block above the first divider is this file's state, one `name: value` per line:
  tree-reads-started                how many times the service began reading a whole tree
  tree-reads-finished               how many of those reads finished and were kept
  tree-reads-failed                 how many of those reads failed
  trees-loaded-from-disk            how many times a tree saved by this same binary was loaded instead of read
  files-read-into-trees             the files all finished reads parsed between them
  judgings-from-the-tree            judgings the service answered from a tree it held
  judgings-told-the-tree-is-coming  judgings told to ask again because a read of the tree was running
  judgings-declined                 judgings the service declined, so the run read the files itself
  judgings-answered-smaller         judgings answered by the rules that read one file only, because the tree could not carry them

Between the dividers is the list: one event per line: the unix time in milliseconds, a tab, what happened, a tab, the checkout or project it was for

Safe to delete — the counts start again from nothing; nothing reads them but tests and a person.

